seline_margin": -6.14, "analyst_adjustment": -0.36, "key_factors": ["R+6 district lean with incumbent Mike Flood's incumbency and 4-to-1 fundraising edge", "Rater consensus of Likely/Solid R, though two raters say Lean or Solid with some spread", "A late Democratic-sponsored poll shows a one-point race, but older polls had Flood up 7-8", "Prediction markets price Flood near 82% to win"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a clear but not overwhelming Flood advantage: a Republican-leaning district, incumbency, and a large money edge, with most raters at Likely R or better. A recent Democratic poll showing a one-point race justifies staying closer to the fundamentals than the rater-implied double-digit margin, but not enough to call this a tossup.", "watch": "Whether independent or late-September polling confirms the tight September Democratic-sponsored survey, or whether the race reverts to the mid-summer margins.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Mike Flood (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Nebraska's 1st District pairs incumbent Republican Mike Flood against Democrat Chris Backemeyer in a seat that leans Republican by about six points. Flood holds a large fundraising advantage, with roughly $2.4 million raised and $1.6 million on hand, while Backemeyer has raised about $590,000. Most expert raters place the race in the Likely or Solid Republican column, though a couple are less emphatic.\n\nPolling is thin and mixed. A Democratic-sponsored September poll of likely voters showed a one-point race, but earlier surveys from the spring had Flood ahead by seven to eight points. Prediction markets price Flood's win probability around 82 percent. The race has drawn attention through a well-covered debate over Iran, tariffs, Medicaid and immigration, but the structural advantages still favor the incumbent.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1466, "margin": -6.26}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1237, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "